Shin Splints (Medial Tibial Stress Syndrome)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Diffuse medial tibial pain, Exacerbation with running/impact, Tenderness >5cm along bone

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-055
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of lower leg pain causing a sudden inability to walk without a severe limp following an abrupt increase in athletic training.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a young adult who developed severe, unrelenting lower leg pain after a long run, rendering them unable to bear weight normally. Urgent assessment was required to definitively differentiate severe medial tibial stress syndrome (shin splints) from an acute, high-risk tibial stress fracture or acute exertional compartment syndrome, and to implement immediate rest and mechanical offloading prior to daytime sports medicine review.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The inside of my shins are throbbing and burning; I tried to push through my run, but the pain became so severe I had to stop, and now every step is agony."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute, functionally limiting flare of bilateral lower leg pain (worse on the right). Timeline: Patient recently (2 weeks ago) began training for a marathon, representing an abrupt, massive increase in training volume on hard surfaces. Initially experienced a diffuse, dull ache localized to the distal two-thirds of the posteromedial tibial border that would warm up and disappear during a run. Today, the pain failed to resolve after a warm-up and progressively worsened into a severe, sharp, burning pain, forcing the patient to abort the run. The dull ache is now constant at rest and consistently provoked by any weight-bearing or walking.
Functional Status: Ambulating with a pronounced limp; highly distressed by the pain and the interruption to training.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of progressing to a complete cortical stress fracture if training is not immediately halted.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 | HR: 85 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, frustrated, resting with both legs elevated.
Physical Exam: Inspection of the lower legs reveals mild, localized soft tissue edema palpable directly over the medial tibial crests bilaterally. Crucially, palpation demonstrates diffuse tenderness extending over a continuous vertical segment greater than 5 centimeters along the posteromedial tibia. There is an absolute absence of severe, pinpoint, focal bone tenderness (the "jump sign" characteristic of a true stress fracture). Dull ache is consistently provoked by resisted active plantarflexion and inversion of the ankle against manual resistance. Biomechanical assessment identifies significant pes planus (flat feet) and excessive dynamic overpronation. Lower leg compartments are soft and compressible; distal pulses and sensation are entirely intact (ruling out compartment syndrome).
Environmental Observations: Worn, unsupportive running shoes visible, confirming the biomechanical risk factor.
Medication Reconciliation: Applied ice and took Paracetamol 1g with minimal relief of the deep periosteal ache.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute, severe Medial Tibial Stress Syndrome (MTSS / Shin Splints) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Tibial Stress Fracture - Ruled out clinically by the diffuse nature of the tenderness (>5cm) rather than an exquisite, localized, pinpoint focal bony tenderness, though the patient is on the spectrum toward developing one.

Acute Exertional Compartment Syndrome - Ruled out by the pain being localized to the bone rather than a feeling of tight, bursting muscle, the soft compressible fascial compartments, and normal distal neurovascular status post-exercise.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out by the bilateral presentation, the clear mechanical athletic trigger, and the pain localized to the bone rather than deep within the calf muscle belly.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, acute-on-chronic exacerbation of MTSS following a classic historical trigger: an abrupt increase in training volume coupled with poor biomechanics (overpronation/worn shoes). The critical clinical imperative was to differentiate this severe periosteal inflammation from a structural tibial stress fracture or compartment syndrome. Clinical examination safely confirmed the diffuse nature of the pain and the absence of compartmental tightness. The severity of the pain resulted in functional impairment (limping) and severe distress regarding training goals. Urgent after-hours assessment provided crucial diagnostic reassurance, ruled out surgical/orthopedic emergencies, and initiated strict, mandatory activity modification to prevent progression to a complete fracture.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Naproxen 500mg BD with food for 5-7 days to aggressively manage the acute periosteal inflammation. Advised absolute, immediate cessation of all running and high-impact activities.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Strictly apply an ice massage (freezing water in a paper cup and rubbing the exposed ice directly along the shin bone) for 10 minutes, three times a day. Consequence: The intense, direct cold rapidly constricts the inflamed blood vessels stripping the bone lining, immediately numbing the sharp, burning pain (positive reinforcement).
Action: Switch entirely to zero-impact cardiovascular exercise (swimming or stationary cycling) for the next 2-3 weeks. Consequence: Maintains the patient's athletic cardiovascular fitness without applying the destructive impact forces to the tibia, allowing the bone to heal without losing training progress (negative reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Mandatory referral to a sports physiotherapist and podiatrist for gait analysis, strengthening, and orthotic intervention.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP or Sports Physician review in 2 weeks. A gradual, highly structured return to running program is required only once completely pain-free on palpation and hopping.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the calf or shin suddenly becomes massively swollen, tight, glossy, and agonizingly painful, or if the foot turns cold and numb (signs of acute compartment syndrome).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical, reflecting severe inflammation of the bone lining. X-rays are typically normal in MTSS. If pain persists despite rest, a bone scan or MRI may be required to rule out a hidden stress fracture. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute burning pain will subside with rest and NSAIDs over 1-2 weeks. However, returning to running without correcting your flat feet and replacing your shoes will result in an immediate recurrence or a true broken bone.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if your lower leg suddenly swells up, feels incredibly tight and bursting, or if you completely lose feeling in your foot.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The muscles in your lower leg are pulling too hard on their attachment to the shin bone, literally causing the lining of the bone to inflame and peel away slightly. This is caused by increasing your running distance too quickly while having flat feet. The anti-inflammatories will calm the chemical fire, but the bone needs complete rest from impact to heal.
Actionable Steps: You must stop running immediately. If you run on this tomorrow, it will likely turn into a true stress fracture. Use the ice-cup massage directly on the bone. You must see a podiatrist to get better shoes and arch supports before you run again.

